<p>President Trump said he would fire Federal Reserve Chair Jerome Powell if Powell does not leave &quot;in time,&quot; escalating a long-running clash over the central bank chief’s future and interest-rate policy. Reports say Powell’s term as Fed chair ends in May, while his term as a member of the Fed’s Board of Governors runs until 2028, creating a dispute over whether he can stay at the central bank after stepping down as chair. Trump also said he would not stop a Justice Department investigation involving Powell, according to the Financial Times, adding another layer of pressure to the leadership transition at one of the world’s most important central banks. The stakes reach far beyond Washington: the Federal Reserve’s independence, leadership continuity, and rate decisions shape borrowing costs, investment conditions, and global financial confidence.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/1Ys5dqazkQ-Z9fKwWW7cRmUNLHi3x8We6vzO78Ggo28Yrl9Y5aVYYyC3QSOISZxDM4qBzOXzPXrHSZotemqhgvnAwndoel-qQ0W2q_tiJXP3wXhzd67Mgbc09aJrwdHBh5zzD_6MebSMlEEVdMlO50Ptai-kZMFOZgmR2t_yeTFd-Yqrq-Jcg2J3N_GidSbQcYdBHBQLWF_3zA4xHLFuN-ppMDB-TNMgR9BBPmrQ1sJnuNIFW_H2N0pbsINVN3hi3R2tac8LOOGoyzs1u-xl' alt='Image accompanying live coverage as Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent says he is confident Fed chair nominee Kevin Warsh will be confirmed.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Successor named: Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said he was confident Trump’s nominee, Kevin Warsh, would win confirmation to replace Powell, signaling that the administration is preparing for a formal handover at the Fed.</li><li>Interview setting: Trump made the threat in an interview with Fox Business that several outlets cited, underscoring that he delivered the warning publicly rather than through a formal White House statement.</li><li>May deadline: Al Jazeera reported that Trump pointed specifically to May 15 as the date by which Powell should vacate his position, giving the confrontation a near-term deadline.</li><l